speechtext:
When it comes to the
expenditure of money I am no good at all;
I leave it to others.

He was not a white man;
he was an Indian. I went up chat river, but in order to do so I lmd to pay $60 out of my own pocket. No hon. member here representing a city like Ottawa, or Montreal, or Toronto, or Vancouver, can realize what it means for a member of parliament to have to travel over practically half an empire in extent and under very difficult conditions m order to come into contact with the people who are working against difficulties. Ontario and Quebec for hundreds of years back were known as Canada; indeed, it is not many years ago when the word "Canada" meant Ontario, Quebec and the maritime provinces. In British Columbia we find a young country and we shall find one day that Canada will be proud of the great wealth of that province

Supply-Post Office

and particularly the northern part of it. I believe nothing can stop the development of British Columbia, but to-day what we need to give the people there is good mail service and telephone communication wherever we can possibly do so.
I have on my desk resolutions from the associated boards of trade of British Columbia assembled. They travelled hundreds of miles in order to formulate these resolutions for presentation to the department. I will not read them because I think the Postmaster General knows what they are. What are they asking? They ask, first; that better mail service be inaugurated in the rural districts, and, second, that Prince Rupert east to Edmonton be given a daily mail service.
What has been done by the Post Office Department this year? I find that a daily mail service has been introduced between Quebec city and Cochrane, and the explanation is that on account of the great lumber and mineral resources through which that line passed, it was necessary to put on a daily mail service. There is also a daily mail service into Rouyn. I do not object to these; I think they are essential; but let me direct the attention of the Postmaster General to this: if a daily mail service is essential between Cochrane and Quebec, and into the mineral fields of Rouyn, it is equally important that a daily mail service be inaugurated between Edmonton and the port of Prince Rupert. From Prince George to Prince Rupert the Canadian National travels through, I think, one of the largest mineral, lumbering and agricultural areas in northern British Columbia. Rather than trouble the house with a lengthy description of that section of the country, I will simply read the resolution of the associated boards of trade. This was the result of a long study and visits along the Canadian National line from Prince George west to Prince Rupert. This resolution, which *was sent to the Postmaster General and also to the Minister of Trade and Commerce (Mr. Malcolm), reads:
I beg to inform you the following resolution was duly passed at the last regular meeting of this board of trade:
"Whereas, owing to the inadequacy of the present mail service between Prince Rupert and Edmonton, great inconvenience is caused to merchants, farmers and those engaged in mining and other industries;
and whereas, this inadequate mail service is the cause of serious loss of business and time to all those enumerated above;
and whereas, recent inquiry by a special committee of the Prince Rupert Board of Trade into the whole situation revealed the inadequacy of the present mail service west to and east from Prince Rupert;
and whereas the Prince Rupert Board of Trade has been petitioned by the various 
boards of trade in northern British Columbia to lend their full support to their demand for a daily mail service;
and whereas the Prince Rupert Board of Trade fully realize that the development and best interests of this district urgently demand a daily mail service to and from Prince Rupert
east;
Therefore, be it resolved, that the Prince Rupert Board of Trade urge that the Hon. P. J. Veniot, Postmaster General, take immediate action whereby a daily mail service will be established between Prince Rupert and Edmonton."
As this is a matter of the most vital importance to the welfare of Prince Rupert and the country lying to the east of it, we sincerely trust that you will give the matter your very earnest and immediate consideration, in order that the daily service might be inaugurated upon the resumption of the daily passenger train service.
